Rem ===================================================================
Rem tests/harness.shoddy -- the whole game, plus enough of the turn loop
Rem to drive it without a terminal.
Rem
Rem Every suite in this directory includes this file and nothing else.
Rem What it adds to the game is twelve lines: a Step that takes one turn
Rem the way the loop does, and three small words for asking what a turn
Rem said. That it is only twelve lines is the point being made -- the
Rem loop has nothing in it worth reproducing here, because everything
Rem that decides anything is already a function.
Rem ===================================================================

Include "../mungo-caverns-core.shoddy"
Include "../mungo-caverns-names.shoddy"        As Name

Include "../mungo-caverns-say.shoddy"          As Say
Include "../mungo-caverns-ask.shoddy"          As Ask
Include "../mungo-caverns-state.shoddy"        As State
Include "../mungo-caverns-look.shoddy"         As Look
Include "../mungo-caverns-travel-rules.shoddy" As Travel
Include "../mungo-caverns-curmudgeon.shoddy"   As Curmudgeon
Include "../mungo-caverns-pirate.shoddy"       As Pirate
Include "../mungo-caverns-clock.shoddy"        As Clock
Include "../mungo-caverns-closing.shoddy"      As Closing
Include "../mungo-caverns-hint.shoddy"         As Hint
Include "../mungo-caverns-score.shoddy"        As Score
Include "../mungo-caverns-verb.shoddy"         As Verb
Include "../mungo-caverns-liquid.shoddy"       As Liquid
Include "../mungo-caverns-fight.shoddy"        As Fight
Include "../mungo-caverns-act.shoddy"          As Act
Include "../mungo-caverns-save.shoddy"         As Save
Include "../mungo-caverns-magic.shoddy"        As Magic
Include "../mungo-caverns-turn.shoddy"         As Turn

Rem ---- driving the game ------------------------------------------------
Rem One turn, exactly as the loop takes it apart from the prompting: the
Rem turn counts, the clock ticks, the command runs, and a command that
Rem moved you describes where you have arrived. The loop does that last
Rem part at the top of the following turn rather than here, which makes
Rem no difference to either the game or the order of what is said -- the
Rem only thing left out is the flush, and flushing decides nothing.

Def Step(g As Game, line As String) As Game
    Let d = Command(ClockTick(With(g, Turns = Turns(g) + 1)), line)
    Let settled = Settled(After(d))
    If Redescribes(How(d)) Then
        Shown(settled, True)
    Else
        settled

Rem A forced room moves you straight on again without asking for a
Rem command -- it describes itself and forwards you, which is how
Rem ">>Foof!<<" is seen at all. The loop does this before it prompts, so
Rem a scenario that stopped short of it would be standing somewhere no
Rem player can ever stand.
Def Settled(g As Game) As Game
    If IsPlaying(g) And IsForced(g) Then
        Settled(AfterMove(Move(DescribeOnly(g), HereMotion())))
    Else
        g

Def Steps(g As Game, lines As List Of String) As Game
    Fold(lines, g, Fn(acc, l) => Step(acc, l))

Rem A game with nothing on its output channel, so the next thing said is
Rem the only thing there.
Def Hushed(g As Game) As Game
    With(g, Output = Silent())

Def Spoken(g As Game) As String
    Join(Output(g), "")

Rem What this one command says, and nothing else. (Spoken rather than
Rem Said: Said is the parser's own record of a rewritten command.)
Def Reply(g As Game, line As String) As String
    Spoken(Step(Hushed(g), line))

Def Mentions(text As String, want As String) As Boolean
    Instr(text, want) > 0

Rem The reference seed the recorded games use.
Def Fresh() As Game
    NewGame(1494912171)

Rem In the well house with the lamp, which is where most scenarios start.
Def Outfitted() As Game
    Steps(Fresh(), { "in", "take lamp", "take keys" })

